{% set local_problem %}
<p>I see the same pattern all over Scottsboro: gutters that work fine and downspouts that don't.</p>

<p>Most homes around here — especially anything built since 2000 — have standard 2x3" downspouts. The builder installed them because that's what's cheap and what meets minimum code. But a 2x3" downspout only handles about 600 square feet of roof area before it's overwhelmed. Many Scottsboro homes have 1,000+ square feet draining to a single downspout.</p>

<p>The result: during a real storm (and we get plenty of those), water backs up at the outlets, fills the gutters, and overflows at corners and low spots. The homeowner thinks they have a gutter problem. They actually have a downspout capacity problem.</p>

<p><a href="/recent-projects/">Lake properties</a> have an additional issue: slope. Those beautiful lakefront lots pitch toward the water — which means downspout discharge runs back toward the house unless you route it underground. I've seen basement moisture problems on Goose Pond homes where the only issue was downspouts dumping 6 feet from the foundation on ground that sloped 8 feet back toward the crawlspace. The "drainage system" was actively directing water toward the house.</p>

<p>The fix is usually straightforward: properly sized downspouts (3x4" handles 1,200 sq ft each), adequate quantity (one per 30-40 feet of gutter run), and extensions or underground routing that moves water well away from the foundation — 6 feet minimum, 10+ preferred.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>A properly functioning downspout system has three components: capacity, quantity, and discharge routing.</p>

<p><strong>Capacity:</strong> Your downspouts need to drain water as fast as your roof sheds it. In North Alabama — 55+ inches annual rainfall, peak intensity of 5-6 inches per hour during storms — standard 2x3" downspouts often aren't enough. 3x4" handles twice the volume (1,200 sq ft vs 600 sq ft). Cost difference is maybe $50-75 more per downspout. Cheap insurance.</p>

<p><strong>Quantity:</strong> Rule of thumb is one downspout per 30-40 linear feet of gutter, but that assumes adequate sizing. Valleys and concentration points often need additional downspouts because multiple roof planes dump water into one gutter section.</p>

<p><strong>Discharge routing:</strong> Where the water goes matters as much as getting it off your roof. Splash blocks move water 2-3 feet. Extensions move it 4-6 feet. Underground drains can route it 10-50+ feet — far enough to genuinely solve foundation moisture problems on difficult lots.</p>

<p>For <a href="/recent-projects/">lakefront properties</a> where grades work against you, underground routing is often the only real solution. A 4" buried pipe with proper slope moves water by gravity — no pumps, no electricity, nothing to fail while you're away.</p>
{% endset %}

